From 4f3bc97e39de59736d6a4a5737633a62d9cb7ba1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Samuel Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 21:12:16 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] microblaze: Start threads at the entry point

_CPU_Context_Initialize() sets r15 to the entry point, and
_CPU_Context_switch() returns with

	rtsd    r15, 8

So a new thread starts 8 bytes into _Thread_Handler(), which skips the
first two instructions of its prologue.  With GCC 12.4.1 and -O2 these
are the stack frame allocation and the store of r15:

	900100f4 <_Thread_Handler>:
	900100f4:	3021ffd0 	addik	r1, r1, -48
	900100f8:	f9e10000 	swi	r15, r1, 0

_Thread_Handler() then runs in the 64 bytes which
_CPU_Context_Initialize() leaves above the initial stack pointer.  This
works only as long as its frame fits in them, and the frame described
by its call frame information does not exist.

Set r15 to the entry point minus 8, as the SPARC port does for o7.

Assisted-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 cpukit/score/cpu/microblaze/cpu.c | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/cpukit/score/cpu/microblaze/cpu.c b/cpukit/score/cpu/microblaze/cpu.c
index b922950004..e14cdcb727 100644
--- a/cpukit/score/cpu/microblaze/cpu.c
+++ b/cpukit/score/cpu/microblaze/cpu.c
@@ -70,7 +70,8 @@ void _CPU_Context_Initialize(
   memset( context, 0, sizeof(*context) ) ;
 
   context->r1 = stack_high - 64;
-  context->r15 = (uint32_t) entry_point;
+  /* _CPU_Context_switch() returns with "rtsd r15, 8" */
+  context->r15 = (uint32_t) entry_point - 8;
 
   uint32_t msr;
   _CPU_MSR_GET( msr );
